WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.290
The first trailer for Spider-Man: Far From
Home was pretty amazing and spectacular...but

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.330
it was also stuffed with more MCU and Marvel
Comics tidbits than you can shake a spider-stick

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:09.840
at.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.200
Here are even more small details you may have
missed in the Far From Home trailer.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.930
As excited as they are for all things MCU,
plenty of fans are still kind of annoyed that

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:23.070
Far From Home's trailer has revealed that
so many characters are alive — before we

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.750
get to see what happens in the fight against
Thanos in the Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:27.780
Well...whatever, dude.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.110
The trailer reveals that Cobie Smulders is
back in the role of Nick Fury's Number 2,

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:32.800
Maria Hill.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.040
What'll she be up to in Spider-Man's side
of the MCU?

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.640
Probably fighting giant sand monsters, for
starters.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.800
At the very least, we know that whatever the
Avengers do to win the day in Endgame, Hill

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:46.160
has been un-dusted, right along with Nick
Fury and Spidey himself.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.280
One encouraging detail in the Far From Home
trailer happens before Peter leaves New York

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.280
City.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.579
Before heading out on his trip, he pays a
visit to the new bodega location of the friendly

00:00:55.579 --> 00:01:00.560
Mr. Delmar, whose original store he inadvertently
helped destroy while thwarting a high-tech

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.180
ATM robbery in Homecoming.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:05.880
"Mister Delmar…"

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.840
While he's still the same kindly neighborhood
grocer we saw he was in the first movie, Delmar

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:16.729
clearly hasn't forgotten about the violent
destruction of his original store — and

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:19.700
for whatever reason, he doesn't want his customers
to forget, either.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.860
Up above the register, he's saved some framed
newspapers with headlines and photos about

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:29.159
the incident, including a framed photo of
him doing "go-web-go" hands next to Spider-Man.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.220
Not a bad souvenir...

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.000
While out sightseeing in London, the students
face an attack while in traffic on the Tower

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.010
Bridge.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:40.119
As lightning strikes all around them, Peter's
classmates sprint between buses and cars to

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:40.620
find safety.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.420
As they do, we can see a license plate on
a bus that partially reads "MYS" — which

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:49.270
certainly seems like it refers to the movie's
probable villain, Mysterio.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:54.130
On the back of a nice, shiny Audi, another
license plate reads "2865 SEP."

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:59.520
Now, remember the "Asm 212" we spotted in
Venice, which likely pointed to Hydro-Man's

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.280
first appearance in Amazing Spider-Man #212?

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.820
Well, this license plate seems to reference
another villain's first appearance: specifically

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:13.440
September 1965's Amazing Spider-Man #28: the
debut appearance of the Spidey villain Molten

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.500
Man, who we seem to have glimpsed at a different
point in the trailer.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.720
Think we're done with secret license plate
messages?

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.720
Think again.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:20.720
Sandblasted

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.410
When the giant sand monster rises up to fight
Nick Fury and Maria Hill, we can see the first

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.090
three digits of yet another car's license
plate.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.090
The visible portion of the plate reads "463."

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.379
That seems to reference Amazing Spider-Man
#4, published in September 1963, and which

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:38.940
featured the introduction of the classic Spidey
character Sandman.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.290
We've seen Sandman in the movies before—
he appeared as one of several bad guys in

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.629
Spider-Man 3, played by Thomas Haden Church.

00:02:45.629 --> 00:02:49.480
How will this version of Spidey's sandy scoundrel
differ from the one we've seen before?

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:50.840
We'll know for sure on July 5.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.080
Enough with the license plates, right?

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.300
Spider-Man: ​Far From Home​ manages yet
another stylish shout-out in its first trailer,

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.709
this time specifically for classic Spidey
writer J.M. DeMatteis, who gets a hotel in

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:02.820
Venice​ named after him.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.980
For those who don't know, DeMatteis worked
with most of the major comic book companies,

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:10.430
including writing over 300 issues with Marvel
Comics​ alone.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:15.099
He's responsible for writing some or all of
the most significant Spider-Man stories, including

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:19.600
"Maximum Carnage," the Harry Osborn/Green
Goblin-centric "The Child Within," and perhaps

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.400
most notably, "Kraven's Last Hunt."

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:26.340
During the opening scenes of the US version
of the Far From Home trailer, Peter watches

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:29.690
as Happy Hogan and Aunt May awkwardly flirt
with each other:

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:30.440
"You look nice.”

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:30.940
“Thank you.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:31.440
You too.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.020
“New dress?

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.800
Yeah.. how’d you know?"

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.520
"what just happened?"

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.440
What seems to have just happened is that Happy
and May might already be dating at this point

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.959
in the movie's story...having kept the whole
thing something of a secret from Peter up

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:47.959
until now.

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:51.629
The line of dialogue that really sets off
sirens for that potential plot development

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:56.420
is when Happy compliments May's new dress...but
won't say how he knows it's new in front of

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:56.920
Peter.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.299
It feels like the only way for him to know
the details of May's wardrobe would be if

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:04.100
they were spending a lot of time together,
socially...without Peter knowing.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:07.810
In the past, Tony Stark was always the flirty
one when it came to Aunt May.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.520
"It's so hard for me to believe that you are
someone’s aunt.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.240
“Yeah, well we come in all shapes and sizes,
you know.”

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.440
“This woman’s meatloaf is exceptional."

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.420
"just stop you there."

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.560
But as it turns out, May might have been single
and looking — looking past Tony, and apparently

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:24.130
right at the guy who does all of Stark's gruntwork.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.810
If true — good going, dude.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.440
"I have to tell you, not a fan of that Tony
Stark."

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.300
While the students are resting up at their
hotel after Mysterio steps in to save Venice,

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.470
you can see a small sticker on the lid of
Ned's laptop, featuring a picture from a popular

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.120
webcomic that you may remember going viral
in 2016.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.220
In case you don't recognize the small sticker,
it's the "'This is Fine' dog," from a widely

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:49.960
shared installment in the series Gunshow by
artist KC Green.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.880
The comic, which is basically about being
oblivious and cheerful while your house burns

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.260
down around you, also embraces a philosophy
that we feel would really come in handy if

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.430
we lived in the constantly besieged world
of the MCU.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.580
Despite all the frequent disasters and threats
of annihilation, it still manages to seem

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.200
like a pretty fun place to live.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.500
Dark Elves want to dominate the Nine Realms?

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:10.020
This is fine.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.780
Scarlet Witch blew up a building, sparking
several international incidents?

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:14.500
This is fine!

00:05:14.789 --> 00:05:18.759
The Far From Home trailer kind of sort of
spoiled some parts of Avengers Endgame?

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:20.870
That's… well, that's not ideal.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:21.640
But it's fine!

